        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.351,
            0.846,
            0.39
          ],
          "content": "rectangle and triangles."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.399,
            0.695,
            0.421
          ],
          "content": "？ Consider a trapezium WXYZ with WX ‖ ZY. Find its area."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.369,
            0.427,
            0.631,
            0.539
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_18_image_7.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.552,
            0.828,
            0.572
          ],
          "content": "Construct $\\mathrm { W M } \\perp \\mathrm { Z Y }$ , and $\\mathrm { X N } \\perp \\mathrm { Z Y }$ (WM and XN perpendicular to ZY)."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.573,
            0.375,
            0.596
          ],
          "content": "Is WXNM a rectangle?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.598,
            0.845,
            0.655
          ],
          "content": "$\\angle MWX = \\angle NXW = 90^{\\circ}$ , since WX‖ZY and the interior angles on the same side of a transversal (WM and XN) add up to 180°. Therefore, WXNM is a rectangle."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.153,
            0.654,
            0.244,
            0.671
          ],
          "content": "We have"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.222,
            0.676,
            0.775,
            0.726
          ],
          "content": "\\mathrm{Area} \\mathrm{WXYZ}=\\mathrm{Area} (\\Delta \\mathrm{WZZ})+\\mathrm{Area} \\mathrm{WXNM}+\\mathrm{Area} (\\Delta \\mathrm{XNM})",
          "image_source": "images/page_18_equation_12.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.729,
            0.845,
            0.782
          ],
          "content": "Let us assign letter numbers to the lengths that we need to find the area of the trapezium. Let $\\mathbf { M } \\mathbf { Z } = \\mathbf { \\boldsymbol { x } } _ { \\mathrm { { i } } }$ , WM = XN = h, WX = a, NY = y. So we get W a X"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.788,
            0.447,
            0.907
          ],
          "content": "{ \\begin{array} { r l } { \\operatorname { A r e a } \\operatorname { W X Y Z } } & { = { \\frac { 1 } { 2 } }   h x + h a + { \\frac { 1 } { 2 } }   h y } \\\\ & { = h ( { \\frac { 1 } { 2 } }   x + a + { \\frac { 1 } { 2 } } y ) } \\\\ & { = h ( { \\frac { x + y + 2 a } { 2 } } ) } \\\\ & { = { \\frac { 1 } { 2 } }   h ( x + y + 2 a ) . } \\end{array} }",
          "image_source": "images/page_18_equation_14.jpg"
        },
        {
